WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:06.000
We're now going to take a look at some ways to optimize our materials. And the first optimization

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.400
we're going to talk about isn't an optimization for performance, but it is an optimization for your workflow.

3
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.300
And that's the concept of parent materials. Now,

4
00:00:15.099 --> 00:00:19.739
for those of you who are not aware, parent materials are a way to have

5
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:26.519
something pass traits on down to their children. Kind of like how in a biological family

6
00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:29.539
parents pass traits on down to the children.

7
00:00:29.539 --> 00:00:34.479
So here we have the squares, and they have the ability to be blue, red, or green.

8
00:00:34.479 --> 00:00:39.859
And we can see here that the squares have some children, and the children have the ability to be red, green, or blue.

9
00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:47.000
Now again, very, very simplistic look at how parents pass traits on down to their children.

10
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:52.200
But if we take that and we kind of apply it to our material system, we can get something like this.

11
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:57.500
We have this mat ENVy building parent, and then from that we have these children that were made that were

12
00:00:57.679 --> 00:01:04.060
adjusted, and we have one that looks exactly the same, one that looks slightly different, and one that looks completely different.

13
00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:09.989
We also have this mat ENVy glass parent, and similar, we have one that looks

14
00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:12.980
totally different.

15
00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:18.680
Throughout all of these, actually all the glass ones look completely different than their parent material, and that's okay.

16
00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.459
A couple of the reasons why we have it set up like this. One,

17
00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:27.230
since glass is a special material that we're going to be using in special instances,

18
00:01:27.269 --> 00:01:34.689
and it also can be very expensive to render, we want to make sure that we have a parent material that's dedicated for that,

19
00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:39.120
so that we only use that in those limited areas.

20
00:01:39.459 --> 00:01:45.900
This mat ENVy building parent, we're going to use that probably all over, so we want to make sure that

21
00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:54.859
this material that we create is as optimized as possible because it's going to be used all over the place.

22
00:01:55.719 --> 00:02:00.280
Now, a couple of things to note here.

23
00:02:01.329 --> 00:02:05.489
When you are creating materials, you should, or I

24
00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:10.610
recommend that you disable the automatically set usage flags in the editor option.

25
00:02:10.969 --> 00:02:18.729
The reason for this is it will help save on compile times should somebody accidentally put a material on something that it wasn't

26
00:02:18.969 --> 00:02:21.550
necessarily intended for. For example,

27
00:02:22.169 --> 00:02:27.370
sometimes you might find that the landscape material has use with Niagara mesh on it.

28
00:02:27.729 --> 00:02:35.379
The other thing is that we want to keep our materials as modular as possible to minimize

29
00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:43.199
permutations, and we want to optimize for usage, not convenience. So we want to make sure that our materials

30
00:02:44.199 --> 00:02:47.639
only contain things that we intend to use with them and

31
00:02:48.539 --> 00:02:54.060
not contain things that maybe we might possibly turn on, we're not really sure.

32
00:02:56.060 --> 00:03:00.139
The feature quality switch expression nodes

33
00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:03.060
allow you to

34
00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:12.300
ensure that whatever materials you're creating will behave correctly on a platform that might not necessarily

35
00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:15.500
support a certain feature.

36
00:03:15.759 --> 00:03:20.500
For these nodes, we have a shading path, feature level, and quality level.

37
00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:28.340
And basically what we do is we add these to whatever inputs such as base color or normal or roughness or metallic,

38
00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.340
and then we feed in

39
00:03:30.659 --> 00:03:36.659
what will happen when this material is, say, viewed on the default, deferred,

40
00:03:37.419 --> 00:03:44.099
forward, or mobile shading paths, or if it detects that the feature level is default

41
00:03:44.599 --> 00:03:48.520
ES2, ES3, SM5, or SM6, or

42
00:03:49.479 --> 00:03:55.340
the quality of the project has been changed from default, low, high, medium, and epic.

43
00:03:56.789 --> 00:03:58.789
Some important notes about these,

44
00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:07.449
just like with other static switches, these generate additional shader permutations for every possible combination of settings. For example, if we have

45
00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:11.740
two static switch parameters, or I should say

46
00:04:12.439 --> 00:04:17.040
two quality switches, it's going to create a possible combination of

47
00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:19.439
four,

48
00:04:19.439 --> 00:04:24.620
but it's only going to use the combinations that are used in our materials. So just keep that in mind.

49
00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:32.180
We want to make sure that we are using these in situations where we know some type of effect or some type of advanced

50
00:04:32.459 --> 00:04:35.939
material logic is not going to work on one of our target platforms,

51
00:04:35.939 --> 00:04:41.860
so it is okay for us to pay that additional cost to make sure that the material compiles correctly.

52
00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:50.920
So to wrap things up, we want to use shader path switches for render path specific logic. We want to use feature level

53
00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:54.060
switches for cross-platform materials and

54
00:04:54.660 --> 00:05:02.259
quality switches for scalable performance. We always want to make sure that to monitor permutation counts as every combination of

55
00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:05.220
these nodes will have some type of cost.
